{"name": "bad real 245 words to a bare greeting", "input": "hi <@responder>", "lane": "investigation", "response": {"text": "Still no change to the account, and nothing deployed.", "markdown": "Still no change to the account, and nothing deployed. The conversion code is committed as `ea94ab72`, but only inside task fork `remote-4e2814e2b71e285012054c2d`. I'm in a different fork right now (`remote-0db8890588b42a7a54404e04`), where that commit isn't reachable — so I couldn't re-run its gate.\n\n**Readiness has two blockers, not one.** We diagnosed the missing `tflint`. But every readiness card also flagged that validation \"changed tracked files,\" and that half is still unaddressed — it's the sandbox, not the gate. Ten tracked files with secret-shaped names (5 `*.key`, 5 `*.tfstate` pack fixtures) get swapped for empty read-only decoys in any agent box: 0 bytes in the tree against 86–1704 bytes in `HEAD`, and those ten are exactly the files git reports as modified. The tree is dirty before a gate ever runs, so retrying will keep failing that check even once tflint is installed.\n\nSupporting detail: this box *does* have tflint v0.64.0, matching both `.tool-versions` and the CI checksum pin — so provisioning works in a normal box; the readiness runner just didn't get it. `refs/coop/session-parent` is missing here too, and `./run gate review` requires it (`tools/internal/devtool/gates.go:676`).\n\nSo the readiness environment needs tflint v0.64.0 plus that pinned ref. I've also prepared an optional repo change to rename those fixtures so agent boxes stop shadowing them — that's the durable fix for the dirty-tree half. One gap worth naming: no Emisar tool is exposed in this session, so I couldn't check the live account or whether anything has deployed."}, "want_pass": false, "want_critical_failure": true}
{"name": "bad real 266 words to an eight-word question", "input": "is it save to unlock the terraform workspace?", "lane": "investigation", "response": {"text": "I can't see the lock from here — Terraform Cloud isn't reachable in this session — so I can't tell you what's holding it.", "markdown": "I can't see the lock from here — Terraform Cloud isn't reachable in this session — so I can't tell you what's holding it. The rule that decides it: unlocking is safe when no run is genuinely still planning or applying. If the top run sits in `Applying` with no log progress because whatever was executing it died, that's a stale lock and force-unlock is the normal fix. If a run is really mid-apply, leave it alone — two Terraform processes writing the same state is how it gets corrupted.\n\nWhich workspace do you mean? It changes the answer. `va1-apps` is the one that actually deploys the website, and a lock there would explain the whole thing: CI keeps creating runs that can't execute. `tenant-infra` is production, where the website group sits at zero instances — unlocking that won't move the site either way.\n\nOne detail that fits the stale-lock story: VA1's in-cluster Terraform agent job has 2 lost allocations. If an apply was in flight when one died, TFC can be left holding a lock with nothing behind it. As of 14:19Z the agent has 1 running allocation and the website still shows the same 4 allocation IDs it's had all day, so nothing is rolling right now. Treat that as weak support, not proof — a scheduled agent allocation doesn't mean it's connected to TFC, and an apply could be running without touching `website`.\n\nOnce you unlock, queue a fresh run for the current commit instead of resuming the dead one, then watch the website allocation IDs change. That's the signal it actually deployed."}, "want_pass": false}
{"name": "bad real 01:13Z-shaped channel report closing on a gap", "input": "Run notification for <https://app.terraform.io/app/SME-Tenant/va1-apps|SME-Tenant/va1-apps> <https://app.terraform.io/app/SME-Tenant/va1-apps/runs/run-QmzuDtmYr4z9MwKJ|Run run-Qmzu...", "lane": "investigation", "response": {"text": "VA1's website has started rolling — first movement since Aug 5.", "markdown": "VA1's website has started rolling — first movement since Aug 5. The apply that actually deploys the site is the `va1-apps` one, and it's running now: `run-QmzuDtmYr4z9MwKJ` (CI apply, `1a9ec54a`) reached Applying at 16:31:15Z, and the website's running allocation count stepped 4 → 5 inside the last five minutes against 4 declared replicas. It had held the same four allocation IDs all week.\n\nIgnore the `failed: 22` on that job — it reads 22 at every offset back through 24h, so it's an accumulated counter, not this deploy.\n\nThe CLI-triggered run discarded a minute earlier is what let CI through, so whatever was holding `va1-apps` is clear. It's landed when all four allocation IDs have turned over. One gap: `1a9ec54a` isn't in this session's checkout, so I can't tell you which image tag it ships."}, "want_pass": false, "reply_placement": "channel", "want_reply_placement": "thread", "want_critical_failure": true}
